WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.314
Why does a stock's price go up and down every day?

2
00:00:04.314 --> 00:00:06.454
It's simpler than it looks.

3
00:00:07.354 --> 00:00:13.425
A stock price is just the last price where a buyer and a seller agreed to trade.

4
00:00:13.425 --> 00:00:18.054
Right now, buyers and sellers are balanced, at fifty dollars.

5
00:00:18.954 --> 00:00:20.513
Now, good news.

6
00:00:20.513 --> 00:00:24.255
The company sold more than expected.

7
00:00:24.255 --> 00:00:27.788
Suddenly, more people want to buy.

8
00:00:28.689 --> 00:00:33.726
With so many buyers competing, they offer higher prices.

9
00:00:33.726 --> 00:00:36.695
Sellers notice, and ask for more.

10
00:00:36.695 --> 00:00:40.743
The price rises until enough sellers say yes.

11
00:00:41.643 --> 00:00:43.150
Then, bad news.

12
00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:44.959
A product problem.

13
00:00:44.959 --> 00:00:50.286
Now lots of owners want to sell, and few want to buy.

14
00:00:50.286 --> 00:00:55.713
Sellers must accept lower prices, and the price falls.

15
00:00:56.613 --> 00:01:02.502
At some point, buyers think it's a bargain again, and things balance out.

16
00:01:03.402 --> 00:01:05.255
Nobody sets the price.

17
00:01:05.255 --> 00:01:10.478
It's a tug-of-war between buyers and sellers that never stops.

18
00:01:10.478 --> 00:01:14.522
News, fear, and excitement all pull on the rope.

19
00:01:15.421 --> 00:01:18.628
In the short run, prices follow moods.

20
00:01:18.628 --> 00:01:24.788
In the long run, they tend to follow how well the business actually does.

21
00:01:24.788 --> 00:01:28.416
This is for learning, not financial advice.

